Write a TypeScript module (strict mode, Node 24) that exports

```ts
export function withTimeout<T>(
  task: (signal: AbortSignal) => Promise<T>,
  ms: number,
  options?: { signal?: AbortSignal },
): Promise<T>
```

It runs `task` once, passing it an `AbortSignal` that becomes aborted when `ms` milliseconds elapse or when `options.signal` aborts, whichever happens first. The returned promise:

- settles like the task's promise if the task settles first (a synchronous throw from `task` becomes a rejection, not a throw from `withTimeout`);
- on timeout, rejects immediately -- even if the task ignores its signal and never settles -- with an error whose `name` is `'TimeoutError'`, and that same error is the task signal's `reason`;
- if `options.signal` aborts first, rejects immediately with exactly `options.signal.reason`, and the task's signal is aborted with that same reason;
- if `options.signal` is already aborted when `withTimeout` is called, rejects with its `reason` without calling `task`.

After the returned promise settles, nothing it created may keep the Node process alive (for example, a pending 60-second timer after the task already succeeded). Answer with a single ```ts code block.

Write a TypeScript module (strict mode, Node 24, `lib` set to `esnext`) that exports `createPool<T>(items: readonly T[])`, a pool of reusable items whose leases are released with an `await using` declaration:

```ts
{
  await using lease = await pool.acquire();
  use(lease.value);
} // the item goes back to the pool here, even if the block throws
```

The returned pool object has:

- `acquire()` -- returns a promise of a lease for a free item. When no item is free, the promise stays pending until one is released; pending acquires are served first-come-first-served, and a released item goes directly to the oldest waiter.
- `available` -- the number of free items (a read-only property); `waiting` -- the number of pending acquires.

A lease has a readonly `value: T` and releases its item through the ASYNC disposal protocol (so it works with `await using`). Releasing a lease a second time does nothing, even if its item has since been leased to someone else. Answer with a single ```ts code block.

Write a TypeScript module (strict mode) that exports a method decorator `memoize` for the standard ECMAScript decorators that TypeScript supports by default (the project does NOT enable `experimentalDecorators`). It is applied as `@memoize` to class methods that take exactly one argument:

```ts
class Calc {
  constructor(readonly factor: number) {}
  @memoize times(x: number): number { return x * this.factor; }
}
```

Behaviour: the first call with a given argument runs the method (with the correct `this`) and caches the result; later calls with the same argument return the cached result without running it. Arguments are compared the way `Map` keys are (object identity; `NaN` equals `NaN`; `undefined` is a valid key). Each instance has its own cache -- two instances never share results. The decorated method keeps its parameter and return types. Applying `@memoize` to a method with two or more parameters must be a compile error. Answer with a single ```ts code block.

Write a TypeScript module (strict mode) with a typed, immutable HTTP request builder. Export:

- `type Method = 'GET' | 'POST' | 'PUT' | 'DELETE'`
- `type RequestSpec = { url: string; method: Method; headers: Record<string, string>; body?: string }`
- `request()` -- returns an empty builder.

A builder has chainable methods, each returning a NEW builder (earlier builders are never modified, so one partially built builder can be branched into several requests):

- `url(url: string)` and `method(m: Method)` -- each may be called at most once, in either order;
- `header(name: string, value: string)` -- any number of times; a later value for the same name replaces the earlier one;
- `body(body: string)` -- only after `method(...)` has been called with a method other than `'GET'`; may be called again to replace the body;
- `build(): RequestSpec` -- returns a fresh object with the url, method, a copy of the headers, and `body` only if `body()` was called (otherwise no `body` key at all).

These rules must be enforced at COMPILE time: calling `build()` before both `url` and `method` have been set, setting `url` or `method` twice, calling `body()` before `method()` or after `method('GET')`, or passing an unknown method are type errors. Answer with a single ```ts code block.

